    "author": "1900241349175132160",
    "displayName": "1900241349175132160",
    "text": "Mortgage rates soar to 6% \nThe average interest rate for a new five-year fixed mortgage has reached 6%, the highest in three years. This follows rising costs for lenders amid global economic uncertainty, particularly since the Iran war began. According to Moneyfacts, around 1,500 mortgage deals priced below 5% have disappeared since early September. Two-year fixed mortgages are now averaging 5.98%, the highest level since December 2023. Rachel Springall, finance expert at Moneyfacts, warned: \"Average fixed mortgage rates rising back to three-year highs will be disastrous news for borrowers.\" Ian Harris, president of the estate agents' body NAEA Propertymark, said: \"We are seeing first hand how sensitive buyers are to mortgage rates, and the rapid disappearance of sub-5% deals will inevitably add further pressure to affordability.\"\nThe Daily Telegraph \u00a0 Daily Mail \u00a0 Daily Mirror \u00a0 BBC News \u00a0 City A.M. \u00a0 Daily Express \u00a0 The Guardian \u00a0 The I \u00a0\nhttps://t.co/3QcyLXKtp1",
    "createdAt": "Tue Oct 06 14:58:08 +0000 2026",
    "url": "https://x.com/finconnect99/status/2107485564991676538"
  }
